<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What a `.kmpkg` is, told to the desktop.

  Installed by the `.deb` into /usr/share/mime/packages/, where `update-mime-database` reads it. The
  desktop entry beside this one says which program opens the type; this says the type exists. Both
  are needed, and the second is the one that is easy to forget: without it, a file manager has no
  name, no icon and no default application for a `.kmpkg`, and never looks at the `MimeType=` line
  that would have told it.

  `x-` on the type because it is not registered with IANA and is not going to be. It names the
  format rather than a program, unlike the package builder's, because a package genuinely is a
  format: several programs in this repository read one, and `km-pack` writes them.

  `src/register.rs` compiles this very file in and writes it under XDG_DATA_HOME for the tarball,
  which installs nothing. The two are the same bytes and differ only in where they land: a per-user
  definition and a system one for the same type do not conflict, and one of the two is always the
  answer depending on how the machine got here.

  The icon name matches the theme entry the package installs under
  /usr/share/icons/hicolor/<size>x<size>/apps/karaokemachine.png, so a package file in a file manager
  wears the mark of the thing that opens it.

  The magic match is what makes the name reliable rather than merely usual: a package renamed on the
  way through a phone or a mail client is still recognized, and a file that only happens to end in
  `.kmpkg` is not. It is the first eight bytes of the container, which is a format of this project's
  own. See `A package is not an archive a file manager can open` in docs/decisions/packaging.md.
-->
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">
  <mime-type type="application/x-km-package">
    <comment>Karaoke song package</comment>
    <icon name="karaokemachine"/>
    <glob pattern="*.kmpkg"/>
    <magic priority="60">
      <match type="string" value="KMPKG\x1a\0\0" offset="0"/>
    </magic>
  </mime-type>
</mime-info>
